f 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1061 618754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "622390da-a9e6-55af-8449-1ceed5d4cec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1061 618755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddd7ced1-9fe0-533d-bd7a-9dfbcff663f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130305 #edb/ref 1061 618758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f4e64a8-3397-5e7e-b805-080c59c56440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that when we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi y \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we have a parametrically defined curve: \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in \\left[t_1,t_2\\right].\n$$\n\nIn the case of a parametric curve, the arc length element $\\textrm{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following formula for the surface area generated by rotating a parametrically defined curve about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1065 6113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "36a57433-1c78-58b8-860c-0ea6e3471761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1064 "When we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi x \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in the case of a parametrically defined curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1065 6145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "548401c3-a52c-5444-b834-57c50c4bfa72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1067 "Surface Area Generated by Functions of Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1065 7707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186166092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166088 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e99f304d-23f7-55f8-b5bf-1b40e7bbc41d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166089 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166089 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166089 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bbfe0ccc-b157-5117-bcab-cb594271efcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166090 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166090 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166090 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9e887c8-e3c3-59f6-9e0e-6148417fbb0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166091 #edb/ref 1080 "0.1853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166091 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166091 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid